WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.484
Can a fraction change its name without changing its size?

00:00:04.134 --> 00:00:07.272
Today we are exploring equivalent fractions.

00:00:07.492 --> 00:00:12.020
We will split and regroup equal parts, then check our thinking with pictures.

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:15.731
Could one half really be the same amount as four eighths?

00:00:16.381 --> 00:00:18.029
Start with one half.

00:00:18.249 --> 00:00:21.786
Split every part of the whole into two equal pieces.

00:00:22.006 --> 00:00:24.918
The shaded half now contains two quarters.

00:00:25.568 --> 00:00:27.869
Split every quarter in half again.

00:00:28.089 --> 00:00:30.878
Now four of eight equal pieces are shaded.

00:00:31.098 --> 00:00:34.693
One half, two quarters and four eighths are equivalent.

00:00:35.343 --> 00:00:37.471
The number rule matches the picture.

00:00:37.691 --> 00:00:42.005
Multiply the numerator and denominator by the same non-zero number.

00:00:42.655 --> 00:00:46.135
For three fifths, multiply both numbers by four.

00:00:46.355 --> 00:00:50.195
Three times four is twelve, and five times four is twenty.

00:00:50.845 --> 00:00:55.068
Equivalent fractions also land at the same point on a number line.

00:00:55.288 --> 00:00:57.482
They are different names for one number.

00:00:58.132 --> 00:01:00.516
Simplifying works in reverse.

00:01:00.736 --> 00:01:05.230
Group equal pieces together instead of splitting them into smaller pieces.

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:07.970
Group the eighths in pairs.

00:01:08.190 --> 00:01:10.752
Six eighths becomes three quarters.

00:01:10.972 --> 00:01:14.344
We divided the numerator and denominator by two.

00:01:14.994 --> 00:01:16.816
Try twelve eighteenths.

00:01:17.036 --> 00:01:20.324
Both twelve and eighteen divide by six.

00:01:20.544 --> 00:01:25.292
Twelve divided by six is two; eighteen divided by six is three.

00:01:25.942 --> 00:01:30.620
Be careful: adding the same number to both numbers does not preserve the value.

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:33.607
One half and two thirds are different amounts.

00:01:34.257 --> 00:01:36.579
We can also partition a fraction.

00:01:36.799 --> 00:01:40.650
Three quarters is three one-quarter pieces added together.

00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:46.394
Two quarters make one half, so three quarters is also one half plus one quarter.

00:01:46.614 --> 00:01:49.675
Different partitions can describe the same amount.

00:01:50.325 --> 00:01:53.362
Is six eighths equivalent to three quarters?

00:01:57.362 --> 00:01:58.392
Yes.

00:01:58.612 --> 00:02:02.373
Dividing both six and eight by two gives three quarters.

00:02:03.373 --> 00:02:06.262
Equivalent fractions name the same amount.

00:02:06.482 --> 00:02:10.803
Multiply or divide both numbers by the same non-zero factor.

00:02:11.023 --> 00:02:15.413
A model or number line helps you check that the value stays unchanged.
